Window policy of the host with a 2:3 (portrait) camera, see test_2819 and #2819

A sphere is rendered through a 2:3 camera (24x36 mm aperture) in a 160x120 render buffer, so the camera is taller
than the display window and fit matches its vertical aperture. The render pass window policy is the camera's (fit),
then crop, match vertically, match horizontally and don't conform, then fit with an overscan framing and crop with a
film translate framing. Each image must match CameraUtilFraming::ApplyToProjectionMatrix with that window policy, what
Storm draws. A render settings resolution ignores the host framing and its window policy, the camera matching its
horizontal aperture as before (husk relies on it), and clearing it brings the window policy back.
The scenario only runs with the HdArnoldRendererPlugin render delegate driven like a Hydra host, see
testsuite/common/interactive_usd_test.h. Each render is checked in test.cpp, and the last one (fit, no framing) is
compared against the reference image.

author: cyril.pichard

PARAMS: {'program_env': 'interactive_usd'}
